so these are the eight areas where the  government has issued additional  guidance around mixing and travel  that's because they have high case rates  largely as a result of the more  infectious  indian variant bolton tops the chart  with a rate of 451 cases  per 100 000 people with blackburn with  darwin  second with 281 all are well above the  england average of 23 cases per 100 000.  local authorities say they're not in  lockdown but would a tear system of kobe  destructions  hold back the virus well let's take a  look at what happened last autumn as  cases began to build from a summer low  this was the picture in mid-october much  of the country was in tier one with a  rule of six  or tier 2 which banned indoor mixing  but liverpool and some surrounding areas  had just gone into tier 3 with a ban on  socializing altogether  in the city cases roughly halved from  659 for every 100 000 people  to 317 in three weeks but  elsewhere cases continue to rise until  the national lockdown in november  so tough restrictions may work however  scientists believe success is more  likely if they cover large areas  and of course they have to have the  support of local authorities  the indian variant which lies behind the  current rise in cases has already spread  well beyond the hot spots 97 local  authorities have now had at least five  cases  which may cede new outbreaks and  scientists believe it will shortly  replace the  kent variant as the dominant strain  nationwide  but there are two key differences this  time compared to last autumn  one is surge testing now done by local  public health teams rather than test and  trace  in the sefton area of liverpool is  helped to bring daily cases down  from 36 to just four in less than a  fortnight  and the other is vaccination with every  passing day around  200 000 people get their first dose  another 400 000 get their second  so two doses give good protection  against the indian variant  the slower the spread of the variant the  more time for the rollout  but tiered restrictions are unpopular  and  toxic politically so whatever their  scientific merits  they are unlikely to happen
 

